ctory or pick a filename I'm not certain about. Let me write out the task now—I've been overthinking the file path problem.\n\nFor the scope, I'm torn between implementing just the wc utility or doing both implementation and tests together. The ts task was completed as a single unit with both, but wc is significantly more complex—it needs to handle line counting, word counting, byte and character counts, multiple files, and max line length. Estimating the code, I'd be looking at 250-350 lines total, which exceeds the 150-250 line target per task. So splitting makes sense, and I'll propose just the implementation as the first task.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the task definition: implementing cmd/wc/main.go with the core wc functionality, referencing the PRD, use case, test suite, and the ts utility as a pattern. I need to nail down the PRD file naming—looking at the existing pattern for pkg components like prd001-testutils and prd002-sys, the utility PRDs likely follow a similar scheme, so I'll reference the appropriate PRD directory without getting too specific about the exact number.\n\nI'm also checking whether magefiles is a blocker here. The magefiles utility provides the build and lint commands, and the stitch agent needs wc to function, so understanding that dependency chain matters for sequencing.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully — the stitch agent can use `go build` directly without needing mage, so magefiles isn't a hard blocker for implementing wc itself. The magefiles use case is really about orchestrator integration, which is separate infrastructure. I'll move forward with wc as the next task.\n\nGenerating the task definition... I need to handle multiple input sources—reading from files when provided, falling back to stdin if no files are given, and treating the dash as a stdin indicator. The output formatting needs to match GNU wc's right-aligned columns with filenames appended when processing multiple files and a totals line at the end. For error handling, I should exit with code 1 if any file fails to open or read, print per-file errors to stderr, but continue processing the rest. I'm following the pattern from the ts utility: a single main.go file using Go's standard flag package, with main() delegating to a run function that returns an error.\n\nFor reading, I'll use bufio.Scanner or bufio.Reader from the standard library without any external dependencies. Words are counted using the same logic as GNU wc—maximal sequences of non-whitespace characters determined by Unicode's IsSpace function.\n\nThe acceptance criteria cover building and vetting the code successfully, producing output that matches GNU wc's format on test files, and handling edge cases like multiple files and stdin input correctly.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a constraint issue—I'm supposed to anchor tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs, but those IDs aren't provided in the project context. The constraint expects consistency across runs with the same PRD and release, but without those IDs defined, I can't properly structure the tasks as required.\n\nI should remove the speculative design decision about word counting definitions since I haven't actually read the PRD, and instead reference it generically in the required reading section. That way the stitch agent can derive the actual requirements directly from the source material rather than working from my assumptions. and let the stitch agent handle the details. I'll keep design decisions to architectural patterns only (from ARCHITECTURE.yaml and existing code patterns).\n\nLet me also reconsider whether D1 (follow cmd/ts pattern) is appropriate. The architecture says each cmd/ package has main.go. The ts implementation exists and is a good pattern reference. So D1 is derived from the architecture.\n\nD2 (use bufio from stdlib) — this is a reasonable implementation choice derived from the technology choices in the architecture (Go standard library). The architecture says \"only external